more concerns from specialists about the  hybrid learning transition plan  the clark county school district  released the updated 235 page guide just  yesterday brian 8 newshour reporter  kristen drummond shows us the issues  when pre-k  to third grade goes back to the  classroom on the first of march  all elementary students will have access  to specials each day  those classes include art music library  physical education and  humanities but some specialists question  their safety and responsibilities when  back in buildings i think this is a  logistical nightmare rachel frederick  teaches humanities at decker elementary  school  her lessons focus on computer science  but she wonders about her position  when hybrid learning begins march 1st  it's confusing like nobody can really  make sense of that chart  this page in the guide highlights  specialist jobs it says  they'll be assigned one classroom per  grade level to provide instruction  aligned with their specific assignment  meaning the likelihood of visiting  multiple classrooms to teach  there's just an equity issue the clark  county education association notices the  concern and tells me  the teachers union is working on an  agreement with ccsd specific to  specialists  a statement says in part under the  hybrid plan they will be exposed to  multiple students in more than one  classroom  we are asked to be seeing more  students than any other certified  teacher the expectation not only  includes teaching  live in a class but live streaming the  lesson to other classrooms in the grade  level  and to those participating in distance  education it's going to be really  challenging like how do you give that  individual attention  but the biggest frustration for  librarian nikki beilina streets and her  colleagues  being required to supervise classes  during teachers prep  time we're in essence being asked to  babysit  students adjustments when transitioning  from home to campus  kristin drummond 8 news now
 

